A||N||7||Tone007||86 Fiero 2M4 Questions||||5||
Z||000000||Tone007||10-10-2000||02:15 PM||||Hi, I have the opportunity to purchase the above mentioned car with only 26,000 miles on it. The car was stored for about 10 years, and was recently put back on the road. Condition is great, everything works. However, I am a little worried about all these service recalls I've been reading about lately. Was '86 a good year for the Fiero? I don't know if this car has had any recall work done on it, and I'm wondering what kind of problems may be hiding.<P>Anybody know anything about this?<P>Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||deceler8||10-10-2000||03:46 PM||||As to recalls, check with your Pontiac dealer to ensure all your recalls are done.<P>As to your car, time is as much an enemy of auto parts as actual use. Expect most of the rubber parts (hoses, bushings, belts, plug wires) to need replacement. Also cap/rotor and plugs.<P>Of course, change all fluids including brake fluid/clutch (if manual).||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||10-11-2000||09:15 AM||||Every year was a great year for the Fiero, Tone!<P>Seriously, GM ironed bugs out of the Fiero with every successive model year - there were quite a few electrical problems with the 84's that later Fieros don't seem to have.  Most of the cars that caught fire were 84's, also, and there were fewer than 500 fires out of total production of about 330,000, so don't let anybody convince you you're buying a rolling BBQ.<P>I don't know anybody personally with an 86 Coupe, but I know several people with 87's and 88's who've had good luck with them (myself included).  I also had an 86 SE and my father-in-law has one too - they've been great cars, but they're both V6's.<P>Tell us about it.  What color?  Options?  How much $$$ are they asking?||||reg||14
Z||000003||Tone007||10-11-2000||10:05 AM||||The Fiero is silver, which happens to be one of my favorite colors for a Fiero.  It looks a whole lot like this one (same wheels too): <BR> <A HREF="http://fiero.cnchost.com/coupe/85coupe1.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cnchost.com/coupe/85coupe1.jpg</A> <P>It's an auto, has A/C that needs recharging, power windows, and the original stereo which works really well.<P>The guy selling it was asking $2000, he went down to $1700 though.||||reg||14
Z||000004||ChadMan||10-11-2000||11:19 AM||||Christ Almighty!!!  $1700 for a Fiero in excellent condition with only 26K miles is a steal - even if it is "just" an Iron Duke.  It ought to provide you with years and years of fun, reliable daily transportation.  Good find!<P>How did it come to have only 26K miles on it?  And why is the guy selling it now?||||reg||10
Z||000005||Tone007||10-11-2000||11:25 AM||||Well, the guy says the car was repossessed when it was a couple of years old, and was stored until recently.  I checked on CarFax, it's got probably the cleanest record of any car I've seen of it's age.  One title in 1986, with 15 miles on it, the next title in 1997 with 26,000 on it.  The car was originally sold in the town right next to where it is for sale.  About the only problem with it is the headliner is sagging, which is always a lot of fun (not) to fix.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tone007||10-11-2000||02:40 PM||||OK, well, I went back over to see the Fiero again, and it looks too good to pass up, so I left the guy a deposit and am picking the car up tomorrow.<P>This will be my first Fiero.||||reg||6
Z||000007||Gold-86SE||10-11-2000||02:42 PM||||<BR>Congrats!<P>When you get it, we want pictures, we want pictures, we want pictures...<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.txconnect.com/home/rharris/signature-a.jpg"> ||||reg||1
